## By hand

`chain.json` lists every record ID from seq 0 to {{ plan.max_seq }}; repeat the steps for each
`<id>`. Example record used below: `{{ example.id }}` (seq {{ example.seq }}).

### 1. Content, payload, manifest

```bash
{% if has_body %}
sha256sum content/<id>/body.md     # compare with content.body.sha256 in payloads/<id>.json
{% else %}
sha256sum content/<id>/body.md     # (fully disclosed entries only) compare with content.body.sha256 in payloads/<id>.json
{% endif %}
sha256sum content/<id>/files/*     # each must match content.files[].sha256
sha256sum payloads/<id>.json       # compare with payload_sha256 in manifests/<id>.json
sha256sum manifests/<id>.json      # the result must equal <id>
```

Also check the chain: in every `manifests/<id>.json`, `seq` counts up from 0 without gaps and
`prev` is the ID of the record before it.

### 2. Signature

Needs OpenSSL 3 (macOS's LibreSSL may not support it; `brew install openssl@3`).

```bash
echo -n <id> | xxd -r -p > id.bin
openssl pkeyutl -verify -pubin -inkey identity/public_key.pem -rawin -in id.bin -sigfile signatures/<id>.sig
```

`identity/public_key.pem` must be the key written in the genesis record (seq 0):
the `public_key` field of `payloads/{{ plan.chain[0].id }}.json`.

### 3. Third-party timestamp (RFC 3161)

```bash
openssl ts -verify -data manifests/<id>.json -in proofs/<id>.tsr \
  -CAfile certs/freetsa-cacert.pem -untrusted certs/freetsa-tsa.crt
```

Before trusting `certs/freetsa-cacert.pem`, compare its fingerprint with the one FreeTSA
publishes (`openssl x509 -in certs/freetsa-cacert.pem -noout -fingerprint -sha256`); the
expected value is `{{ meta.trusted_tsa_roots['freetsa-cacert.pem'] }}`.

### 4. Bitcoin anchor (OpenTimestamps)

```bash
pip install opentimestamps-client
ots verify manifests/<id>.json.ots     # the .ots file sits next to the manifest
```

Full verification needs a Bitcoin node (a pruned node is enough); otherwise compare the block
header with at least two public block explorers. A pending anchor is not a failure.

## What a successful check means

It shows that each record existed no later than its verified time, has not been changed
since, the order of the records, and that one signing key signed them all. It does not show
who the author is, that they were first, why they made a change, or that the content is
correct.
